Z||000000||fropuff||01-27-2000||10:13 PM||||Does anyone know how many miles a Fiero can go without having any major problems to the trasmission or engine?  Thanks.||||reg||10
Z||000001||Rodrv6||01-27-2000||10:54 PM||||This is a tough question!  There is really no way to give an answer that will be accurate since so much depends on how well the car is maintained, how it's driven, and a whole lot of other variables.  I've seen several cars (one was an 85 Fiero) that have needed major engine work at less than 100,000 miles because they were neglected or driven very hard.  I have an 84 Fiero that I have owned since new.  It has always had regular oil changes, tune ups, and generally good care.  It has 325,000 miles on it, mostly highway speeds.  The engine had 190,000 miles when I decided to overhaul it even though I wasn't really having any problems with it.  Aside from needing some valve guide work I found nothing wrong inside!  I didn't even need to have the cylinders bored, just honed and reassembled with standard size bores and standard size crankshaft bearings.  It all depends on the car's history.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Harry||01-27-2000||11:00 PM||||I cant be certian as I am the third owner, however from what I was told and by the looks of what Ive seen in my engine compartment I would say they were correct.<BR>Mine has 235,000 and has never had an engine or tranmission change since it was built in 85. Yes it needs some/a head job soon (smokes just on start up) but everythings in it's coeect place and all looks original. I wish it did look newer..<BR>Rod 325+ Your killing me dude. I thought mine was high!||||reg||6
Z||000003||mshill||01-28-2000||02:59 PM||||Rodrv6,  my 86SE V6 has 190K on it and I needed to repair the front manifold, so I dropped the whole cradle and disassembled the engine to find the same thing... valve guides a hone and a valve grind.  I am starting to put it back together tonight.<BR>||||reg||10
